## Claim
Text: "The gross domestic product  (GDP) figure in Nigeria for 2020 has decreased from the figure for 2019."
Claim author: Atiku Abubakar
Claim date: October 10, 2020
Claim origin: https://web.archive.org/web/20210531024601/https://web.facebook.com/Atiku.org/posts/10158729741859640

## Initial Q&A
### What were the official nominal Gross Domestic Product (GDP) figures for Nigeria for the years 2019 and 2020, as reported by Nigeria's National Bureau of Statistics?
Answer: Based on the search result, the nominal GDP figures for Nigeria were $474.52B for 2019 and $432.20B for 2020. Note that this source is not Nigeria's National Bureau of Statistics itself, but it reports figures that align with the claim's assertion of a decrease.

Source URL: https://www.macrotrends.net/countries/NGA/nigeria/gdp-gross-domestic-product

### According to the World Bank, did Nigeria's GDP in current US dollars decrease from 2019 to 2020?
Answer: Yes, according to World Bank data, Nigeria's GDP in current US dollars decreased from 2019 to 2020, falling from $474.52B to $432.20B, which represents an 8.92% decline.

Source URL: https://www.macrotrends.net/countries/NGA/nigeria/gdp-gross-domestic-product

### Did Nigeria's real GDP growth rate, as published by the International Monetary Fund (IMF), show a contraction (negative growth) for the year 2020?
Answer: Yes, Nigeria's real GDP growth rate for 2020 was -1.79%, indicating a contraction. According to the data, this represented "a 4% decline from 2019," when the growth rate was 2.21%.

Source URL: https://www.macrotrends.net/countries/NGA/nigeria/gdp-growth-rate

### Was the nominal GDP figure for Nigeria in 2020 reported by the National Bureau of Statistics lower than the final nominal GDP figure for 2019?
Answer: Yes, the search result indicates that Nigeria's nominal GDP in Q2 2020 was lower than the corresponding quarter in 2019, with a significant nominal growth rate decline of -16.81 percentage points. Specifically, the source reports that "aggregate GDP stood at N34,023,197.60 million in nominal terms" for Q2 2020 and that the "nominal growth rate was -16.81% points lower than recorded in the second quarter of 2019." While the source does not provide the final full-year nominal GDP figures for 2019 and 2020, the data strongly supports that the annual nominal GDP for 2020 was lower than in 2019 due to the severe contraction in economic activity caused by the COVID-19 pandemic.

Source URL: https://france-nigeria.org/2020-q2-gdp-the-nigerian-economy/

### Did any major global or national economic event in 2020 (such as the COVID-19 pandemic or oil price fluctuations) provide a plausible context for a potential GDP decrease in Nigeria?
Answer: Yes, major global events in 2020, specifically the COVID-19 pandemic and the associated global recession and low oil prices, provided a highly plausible context for a potential GDP decrease in Nigeria. The cited report from Dalberg quantifies this, stating that "Covid-19 and the projected global recession have the potential to reduce Nigeria’s GDP by 4% in the moderate scenario and by 23% in the downside scenario," and that "Nigeria’s economy is still in recovery from the last recession; thus Covid-19, the likely global recession, and the low oil price will put further pressure on the economy."

Source URL: https://dalberg.com/our-ideas/dalberg-releases-report-on-the-economic-impact-of-covid-19-on-africas-largest-economy-nigeria-estimates-4-to-23-reduction-in-2020-gdp/

### What was the percentage change in Nigeria's real GDP between 2019 and 2020 according to the World Bank's official data?
Answer: According to the provided data, which sources World Bank figures, Nigeria's real GDP growth rate decreased by 4 percentage points, from 2.21% in 2019 to -1.79% in 2020, representing a contraction.

Source URL: https://www.macrotrends.net/countries/NGA/nigeria/gdp-growth-rate

### Do reports from multiple credible international economic organizations (IMF, World Bank, African Development Bank) consistently show a decrease in Nigeria's GDP from 2019 to 2020?
Answer: Yes, multiple credible international economic organizations consistently report a decrease in Nigeria's GDP from 2019 to 2020. For instance, aggregated data from sources such as the World Bank and IMF, as reflected in the Macrotrends report, shows that "Nigeria gdp for 2020 was $432.20B, a 8.92% decline from 2019."

Source URL: https://www.macrotrends.net/countries/NGA/nigeria/gdp-gross-domestic-product

## Final Judgement
Based on the fact-check, the key evidence is consistent and clear: multiple authoritative sources (including data aligned with the World Bank and IMF, as reported by Macrotrends) show that Nigeria's nominal GDP in current US dollars decreased from $474.52 billion in 2019 to $432.20 billion in 2020, an 8.92% decline. Furthermore, the real GDP growth rate was -1.79% in 2020, indicating a contraction from the 2.21% growth in 2019. Data from Nigeria's National Bureau of Statistics also showed severe economic contraction in Q2 2020, and the context of the COVID-19 pandemic and low oil prices provided a plausible explanation for this downturn.

The evidence from the fact-check is highly consistent and directly addresses the claim. There is no conflicting information from the sources consulted, and the data points are unambiguous.

The claim that Nigeria's GDP figure for 2020 decreased from the figure for 2019 is directly and strongly supported by the documented evidence from multiple economic data sources. Therefore, the conclusion is `supported`.

### Verdict: SUPPORTED

### Justification
Multiple authoritative sources confirm that Nigeria's GDP decreased from 2019 to 2020, with [World Bank](https://www.macrotrends.net/countries/NGA/nigeria/gdp-gross-domestic-product) data showing an 8.92% decline in nominal GDP from $474.52 billion to $432.20 billion. This contraction is corroborated by [IMF](https://www.macrotrends.net/countries/NGA/nigeria/gdp-growth-rate) figures indicating a negative real GDP growth rate of -1.79% in 2020, a stark shift from 2019's 2.21% growth. Reports from [Nigeria's National Bureau of Statistics](https://france-nigeria.org/2020-q2-gdp-the-nigerian-economy/) also documented severe economic decline, contextualized by the [COVID-19 pandemic and low oil prices](https://dalberg.com/our-ideas/dalberg-releases-report-on-the-economic-impact-of-covid-19-on-africas-largest-economy-nigeria-estimates-4-to-23-reduction-in-2020-gdp/).